WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.880
Obviously, we've spent a pretty good amount
of time around here talking about the assault

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.160
on free speech on college campuses.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.350
As I've said before, I believe this strike
against free speech to be one of the biggest

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.440
problems we face today as we are breeding
a generation of young people who won't be

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.470
equipped to use logic and reason in their
own arguments when they graduate to the real

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:25.470
working world.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:30.539
The university's misguided use of safe spaces
and trigger warnings, coupled with violent

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.820
protests and deplatforming or shouting down
speakers is actually robbing many of these

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.620
students of literally the most important thing
they can learn in college...the ability to

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.140
think for themselves.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.190
Instead of graduating as fully equipped adults
ready to fight for what they believe in because

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.879
they know what they believe in they are being
coddled like children who might crumble at

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:54.219
the mere hearing of an idea that challenges
them.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.429
This inability to debate ideas is leaking
out of the universities and into society at

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:00.429
large.

00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:03.989
Celebrities spend all day on Twitter ranting
and raving about topics they don't understand

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:04.989
at all.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:09.210
Comedians, some of whom I know personally
and really like, have become the screeching

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.240
lunatics they're supposed to make fun of.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.110
Politicians talk a lot but don't actually
do a lot, and the rest of us are left here

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.590
trying to make some sense of what's going
on.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:23.990
A great example of this is the Title 9 fiasco
which took root on college campuses over the

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.350
past few years.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.680
Title 9 is part of the Education Act of 1972
which states that "No person in the United

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:37.030
States shall, on the basis of sex, be excluded
from participation in, be denied the benefits

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:43.729
of, or be subjected to discrimination under
any education program or activity receiving

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:45.219
Federal financial assistance."

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:47.700
That sounds pretty good right?

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.330
What we should be going for here is an even
playing field of equal opportunity.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:57.250
Title 9 doesn't guarantee equal outcome, which
is where hard work, perseverance and sometimes

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.610
luck comes into play, but it does guarantee
that whether you're a man or a woman, you

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.770
won't be discriminated based on your sex.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.850
This straight forward law of equal opportunity
changed under the Obama administration when

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.640
in 2011 and 2014 he issued new guidelines
on how schools should deal with cases of sexual

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.150
assault on campus.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.370
While I believe Obama's intentions were good,
the law in effect circumvented our system

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.400
of due process.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.130
The Obama administration's policy used the
preponderance of evidence standard in cases

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.440
of sexual assault— not the beyond reasonable
doubt standard — which reduces the burden

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.800
of proof on anyone accused of sexual assault.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.550
This was a subtle but powerful change in how
we litigate these cases of sexual assault

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:48.480
on campus and some would argue an outright
violation of the accused's constitutional

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.030
rights.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.709
Betsey DeVos, Trump's Education Secretary,
recently rescinded these Obama mandates, and,

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:00.060
judging by the celebrity and comedian critics
out there, Trump and Devos were not only anti

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.940
woman, but suddenly pro rape as well.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.970
Christina Hoff Sommers wrote an excellent
piece in the Journal of Higher Education explaining

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:14.370
why removing the Obama guidelines was actually
a win for due process and the constitution,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.069
an article I shared with some of the famous
people I saw ranting about Trump's pro-rape

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:20.220
policy.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.970
Of course nobody responded.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.760
We'll link to Christina's article below for
you to read for yourself.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.550
To unpack Title 9 I'll be talking to author
and Professor Laura Kipnis today, as we unpack

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.480
her own story of how Title 9 has affected
her both personally and professionally.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.020
Laura has been in right in the middle of the
Title 9 firestorm and our friends at Learn

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.660
Liberty have sent her our way this week.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:46.420
Knowing what you're talking about is a lot
harder than just screaming about how you feel

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.230
about something, but a good education taught
me it's worth the effort...

